<body><main>
  <h1><i>Mdk</i> TSS-proximal 10-bp ISM</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">An audited single-gene discussion brief · AlphaGenome and Borzoi · {{ generated_at }}</p>

  <div class="summary">
    <strong>Technical summary.</strong> The 1-kb fine-tuned scan identifies a primary
    +463..+475 window whose fixed TES/3′ effect ranks HSC first among the four modeled
    cell heads in both architectures. A nearby +499..+507 window is sequence-sensitive,
    but Borzoi does not rank HSC first there. These are model effect sizes, not estimates
    of statistical significance or biological causality.
  </div>

  <h2>Background and objective</h2>
  <p>We asked whether short sequence perturbations near the <i>Mdk</i> transcription
  start site change the fine-tuned HSC RNA prediction, whether the response is localized
  relative to macrophage, LSEC and cholangiocyte heads, and whether the same sequence was
  already active in the original Borzoi model. The final question is motivated by gRely:
  an ISM prediction is more credible when target activity, regulatory context, output
  concentration and reproducibility agree, rather than when one output track alone is large.</p>

  <h2>Fine-tuned four-cell Browser</h2>
  <p>We replaced successive 10-bp windows across the TSS-centered 1,024-bp interval,
  using a 2-bp stride and three deterministic mononucleotide-preserving shuffles. For this
  cross-cell comparison we fixed the readout to the TES/3′ 1,024-bp RNA output. The ordinate
  is median signed log2(mutant/reference): negative values lower predicted output.</p>
  <div class="figure">
    <img src="{{ browser_figure }}" alt="Four-cell Mdk signed-effect Browser">
    <p class="caption">Figure 1. One panel per cell head; AlphaGenome and Borzoi are
    overlaid with a common symmetric y-axis. Shuffles are perturbation variants, not biological replicates.</p>
  </div>
  {{ window_table | safe }}
  <div class="caution"><strong>Interpretation boundary.</strong> Candidate selection and
  visualization use the same scan. HSC-first therefore describes relative ordering within
  these four heads and this fixed readout; it is not an unbiased validation rate.</div>

  <h2 class="page-break">What the original Borzoi tracks add</h2>
  <p>The original-model analysis used {{ selected_tracks }} selected tracks, of which
  {{ compatible_tracks }} were unstranded or compatible with the negative strand of
  <i>Mdk</i>. They span RNA-seq, CAGE, accessibility, active histone marks and TF/Pol-II
  occupancy proxies. These modalities are summarized separately and are not compared as
  calibrated units.</p>
  <div class="figure">
    <img src="{{ original_figure }}" alt="Original Borzoi track-group effects">
    <p class="caption">Figure 2. Median signed effect by curated original-Borzoi track group
    for the two retained windows. The original tracks provide regulatory context, not a matched HSC replication.</p>
  </div>
  {{ original_table | safe }}
  <p>The primary window shows pre-existing sensitivity across regulatory proxies without a
  uniform liver-RNA decrease. The secondary window has a coherent negative liver-RNA response
  across {{ secondary_negative }}/{{ liver_track_count }} compatible tracks. Conservatively,
  this supports an already responsive local regulatory context and a difference between the
  two nearby modules; it does not show that fine-tuning created a new element or identify its
  active cell type in vivo.</p>

  <h2>Motif scope and the gRely gap</h2>
  <p>The sequence annotation supports an SP/KLF-rich family-level hypothesis for the primary
  interval. Shared PWM grammar does not distinguish a specific SP or KLF factor, and no matched
  HSC occupancy assay was used here.</p>
  <p>Compared with a full gRely-style evaluation, the present study still lacks:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>independent model seeds or experimental replicates to test reproducibility;</li>
    <li>matched HSC expression and chromatin evidence at the analyzed locus;</li>
    <li>a calibrated measure of how concentrated the effect is among all relevant output tracks;</li>
    <li>motif-breaking, motif-preserving and dinucleotide-matched edits that separate grammar disruption from generic sequence replacement;</li>
    <li>an external truth set linking predicted ISM response to MPRA or CRISPR perturbation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Accordingly, +463..+475 is the stronger candidate for targeted follow-up, while
  +499..+507 should remain a secondary, context-sensitive region rather than a second
  cross-model HSC-specific claim.</p>

  <p class="provenance">Analysis contract v{{ contract_version }}. Report rendered only from
  versioned TSV/JSON analysis outputs; no raw parquet is read by this renderer. Maximum
  independent original-summary difference versus the canonical saved matrix:
  {{ max_difference }}.</p>
</main></body></html>
